   </metsHdr>
   <dmdSec ID="DMD_1843_4801">
      <mdWrap MDTYPE="MODS">
         <xmlData xmlns:mods="http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3 http://www.loc.gov/standards/mods/v3/mods-3-1.xsd">
            <mods:mods xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3 http://www.loc.gov/standards/mods/v3/mods-3-1.xsd">
               <mods:extension>
                  <mods:dateAccessioned encoding="iso8601">2026-09-11T15:50:57Z</mods:dateAccessioned>
               </mods:extension>
               <mods:originInfo>
                  <mods:dateIssued encoding="iso8601">2026-05-18</mods:dateIssued>
               </mods:originInfo>
               <mods:identifier type="uri">https://hdl.handle.net/1843/4801</mods:identifier>
               <mods:abstract>In humid tropical environments, deeply weathered soils dominated by oxide mineralogy are traditionally interpreted as phosphorus-depleted systems. This thesis addresses whether phosphorus enrichment in such soils, when developed over phosphate-rich rocks, can be explained by (bio)lithogenic mechanisms. The objective was to elucidate the processes controlling the occurrence, transformation, and redistribution of phosphorus in a highly weathered tropical pedological system, using the Barreiro Alkaline-Carbonatite Complex (Araxá, MG, Brazil) as a case study. Ferruginous duricrusts and soils were analyzed along a catena, with detailed study of a representative profile in the Cascatinha conservation area, integrating physical, morphological (macro- and micromorphological), chemical, mineralogical, and microscopic analyses. Results show that phosphatized ferruginous duricrusts form at advanced weathering stages, marked by Ca and Mg loss and residual enrichment in Fe, Ti, Ba, and P, evolving into a ferruginous-phosphatic system with features of selective dissolution and internal reorganization. Duricrust degradation is the main soil-forming mechanism, promoting fragmentation, partial deferruginization, and reorganization into micromass and aggregates, with redistribution of phosphorus along the profile. Soil thickening occurs through colluviation and renewed pedogenesis over pre-weathered materials, with organic matter incorporation and intense pedobioturbation, forming latosolic-like structures chemically expressed as latosolic Cambisols. Phosphatization follows integrated pathways involving apatite transformation into secondary phosphates, association with Fe and Al oxides, redistribution in the soil matrix, and reintroduction by bioturbation. Phosphatization in Araxá is thus a non-linear bio-lithogenic process in which phosphorus is preserved, transformed, and recycled within a multi-pathway system, demonstrating that highly weathered tropical soils can act as dynamic phosphorus reservoirs rather than solely depleted systems.</mods:abstract>
               <mods:language>
                  <mods:languageTerm authority="rfc3066" />
               </mods:language>
               <mods:accessCondition type="useAndReproduction">Acesso restrito</mods:accessCondition>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Solos – Teor de fósforo</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Solos – Formação</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Intemperismo</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Apatita</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Estrutura do solo – Araxá (MG)</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:titleInfo>
                  <mods:title>Fosfatização (bio)litogênica: estudo do enriquecimento em fósforo em solos do complexo alcalino-carbonatítico do Barreiro, Araxá, Minas Gerais</mods:title>
               </mods:titleInfo>
               <mods:genre>Tese de doutorado</mods:genre>
            </mods:mods>
         </xmlData>
      </mdWrap>
   </dmdSec>
