</metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.ufmg.br:1843/3099</identifier><datestamp>2026-06-23T12:43:19Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1843_6</setSpec><setSpec>com_1843_2</setSpec><setSpec>col_1843_312</setSpec></header><metadata><mets xmlns="http://www.loc.gov/METS/" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" ID="&#xa;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;DSpace_ITEM_1843-3099" TYPE="DSpace ITEM" PROFILE="DSpace METS SIP Profile 1.0" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.loc.gov/METS/ http://www.loc.gov/standards/mets/mets.xsd" OBJID="&#xa;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;hdl:1843/3099">
   <metsHdr CREATEDATE="2026-11-10T09:23:22Z">
      <agent ROLE="CUSTODIAN" TYPE="ORGANIZATION">
         <name>"Repositório Institucional da UFMG"</name>
      </agent>
   </metsHdr>
   <dmdSec ID="DMD_1843_3099">
      <mdWrap MDTYPE="MODS">
         <xmlData xmlns:mods="http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3 http://www.loc.gov/standards/mods/v3/mods-3-1.xsd">
            <mods:mods xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3 http://www.loc.gov/standards/mods/v3/mods-3-1.xsd">
               <mods:extension>
                  <mods:dateAccessioned encoding="iso8601">2026-06-23T12:43:19Z</mods:dateAccessioned>
               </mods:extension>
               <mods:originInfo>
                  <mods:dateIssued encoding="iso8601">2026-03-04</mods:dateIssued>
               </mods:originInfo>
               <mods:identifier type="uri">https://hdl.handle.net/1843/3099</mods:identifier>
               <mods:abstract>The objectiveof the present study was to examine the effect of resistance training performed with two distinct CAM positions on the hypertrophic response along the muscle length. The ascending radius CAM (ARC) is characterized by a smaller radius at the beginning and a larger radius at the end of the concentric action, whereas the descending radius CAM (DRC) presents a larger radius at the beginning and a smaller radius at the end of these phases. A sample of 29 volunteers was divided into a control group (whose participants were only subjected to ultrasound image acquisition, without training or physical testing) and two training groups. During 12 weeks, the volunteers in the training groups performed training three times per week, consisting of 3–5 sets of 7–8 repetitions at 50–60% of one-repetition maximum (1RM), with a duration of 6 s (3:3) and 120 s of rest between sets. Ultrasound images were used to analyze the cross-sectional areas (CSA) of the rectus femoris (RF) and vastus lateralis (VL) muscles at 30%, 50%, and 70% of femur length (FL) before training and after 6 and 12 weeks. The data were analyzed using repeated-measures ANOVA considering the factors group (ARC, DRC, and control), time (pre, 6, and 12 weeks), and region (30%, 50%, and 70% of FL), adopting a significance level of 0.05. The results demonstrated greater increases in the CSA of the RF and VL muscles in the training groups compared with the control group (p &lt; 0.05), which were more evident after 12 weeks. In addition, the DRC group showed greater increases in the CSA of the RF (p = 0.016) and VL (p = 0.005) compared with the ARC group, also more evident after 12 weeks. No differences were observed in the increase in CSA among the evaluated regions (p > 0.175). It is concluded that different resistance torque profiles throughout the range of motion, influenced by CAM positions, distinctly impact the magnitude of muscle hypertrophy without affecting its regional distribution. Based on these findings, strength and conditioning professionals may identifyknee-extension machines with CAMs that present a larger radius at joint angles corresponding to more elongated muscle lengths. These machines may be prioritized in order to promote greater hypertrophic responses in the RF and VL, since muscle hypertrophy is one of the main goals of individuals who engage in resistance training.</mods:abstract>
               <mods:language>
                  <mods:languageTerm authority="rfc3066" />
               </mods:language>
               <mods:accessCondition type="useAndReproduction">Acesso aberto</mods:accessCondition>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Musculação</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Exercícios físicos</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Articulações - Amplitude de movimento</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:subject>
                  <mods:topic>Hipertrofia</mods:topic>
               </mods:subject>
               <mods:titleInfo>
                  <mods:title>Efeito da modulação do torque de resistência ao longo da amplitude de movimento, via formato do CAM, na hipertrofia regional do vasto lateral e reto femoral</mods:title>
               </mods:titleInfo>
               <mods:genre>Dissertação de mestrado</mods:genre>
            </mods:mods>
         </xmlData>
      </mdWrap>
   </dmdSec>
